---
scope: src/service/install/provisionBrain
status: misaligned
description: >
  Plans the brain-layer scaffolding as exactly TWO InitStep items —
  writes nothing to disk directly. Step 1 scaffolds
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` from the bundled template (default
  `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()`), with YAML null at every unwired slot
  in `mcpServerConfig.args`. Step 2 plans the `.mcp.json` brain entry
  derived from the scaffolded brain.aide via parseBrainAide +
  interpolateArgs. The four entry-point artifacts (playbook,
  study-playbook, update-playbook, research) and the brain root
  directory are NOT scaffolded by this module — they are seeded by the
  integration's `aide-config` body section prose at first
  `/aide:brain config` via the brain's own MCP write tools (e.g.
  `mcp__brain__write_note` for the bundled Obsidian default), reading
  the seed-section bytes via parseBrainAide's typed keys
  (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`).
  Takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer; takes no slot-position
  knowledge of any kind. Inputs are the host project root, the MCP
  config path, and the on-disk bundled template bytes — nothing else.
  Invoked from two callers under the rework: `aide_init({ category:
  "brain" })` (fired by `/aide:brain config` for missing-`brain.aide`
  self-heal) and `cli/init` (fired at cold install to scaffold the
  bundled template into a fresh project; cli/init applies step 1 only,
  never step 2). The retired step builders — `buildBrainRootStep`,
  `buildPlaybookStep`, `buildStudyPlaybookStep`,
  `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`, `buildResearchStep` — are gone, and
  restoring any of them under any rename is the structural regression
  this spec forbids.
intent: >
  provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding the AIDE pipeline
  needs as exactly TWO InitStep records and writes nothing to disk
  directly — the install service's apply layer is the only writer.
  The two steps in fixed order are: (1) the brain config file at
  `.aide/config/brain.aide` (the user-owned configuration directory
  inherited from the root spec), scaffolded from the bundled template
  with YAML null at every unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args`;
  (2) the brain MCP server entry under the fixed `brain` key, derived
  from the scaffolded brain.aide via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs
  (and carrying any null entries in args through unchanged). Inputs
  are the host project root, the MCP config path, and the on-disk
  bundled template bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`; this module
  takes NO `brainPath` parameter, NO slot-position knowledge, NO
  awareness of which arg index means what. The four entry-point
  artifacts (playbook, study-playbook, update-playbook, research) and
  the brain root directory are NOT scaffolded by this module — they
  are seeded by the integration's `aide-config` body section prose at
  first `/aide:brain config`, after the user fills the null slot(s)
  and runs sync, by reading the seed-section bytes from brain.aide
  via parseBrainAide's typed keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
  `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and writing each artifact via
  the brain's own MCP write tool (e.g. `mcp__brain__write_note` for
  the bundled Obsidian default) at paths the integration prose names.
  Slot-semantic, file-path, and write-tool-name knowledge ALL live in
  user-owned prose; provisionBrain is backend-blind. The module is
  invoked from two callers: `aide_init({ category: "brain" })` (fired
  by `/aide:brain config` for missing-`brain.aide` self-heal recovery)
  and `cli/init` (fired at cold install to scaffold the bundled
  brain.aide template into a fresh project). Both callers consume the
  two-step output via the apply layer's idempotent presence check on
  brain.aide; cli/init applies only step 1 (sync owns `.mcp.json`
  brain writes downstream), and `/aide:brain config` does not apply
  step 2 directly either — the integration prose wires the MCP entry
  by running sync after the user fills the null slot. The hand-off is
  load-bearing: provisionBrain plans, the agent fills, sync writes.
  The bundled template emits YAML null at unwired slots regardless of
  caller — `args` is typed `(string | null)[]` and YAML null is the
  explicit, structural unwired-slot signal carried by the schema
  itself, never a literal placeholder string. provisionBrain forwards
  the null verbatim through parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs into
  the derived MCP entry's args; sync, downstream, refuses to write
  null-bearing args. The brain.aide body carries six named
  marker-bounded sections per the parent body grammar, partitioned by
  who reads them: two live agent-facing sections (`orientation`,
  `config`) read by `aide_brain` at runtime, and four install-time-
  seed sections (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
  `researchIndex`) read by the integration prose at first
  `/aide:brain config` to seed entry-point artifacts in the brain.
  provisionBrain reads NEITHER category — the seed sections reach the
  brain via integration prose, not via this module's code. All code
  identifiers and prose in this module refer to the brain in
  storage-agnostic terms; the bundled `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`
  helper folder name is the only allowed exception, describing
  bundled template content rather than a code-level dispatch axis.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      "The brain config step plans creation of `.aide/config/brain.aide`
      inside the host project root as its own InitStep (category: brain,
      name: \"Brain config (brain.aide)\"). When absent, the step is
      would-create with a `content` field containing the canonical
      default brain.aide bytes from the bundled template helper
      (default `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()`), with the parent body
      grammar's six marker-bounded body sections in fixed order and
      YAML null at every unwired slot in `mcpServerConfig.args`. When
      present, the step is exists with no content field. The
      `.aide/config/` directory is the host's user-owned configuration
      boundary inherited from the root spec: any file scaffolded into
      it is owned by the user the moment it lands, and provisionBrain
      never returns would-overwrite for any path under `.aide/config/`.
      The invariant is structural — directory-level — not a per-file
      allowlist for brain.aide alone."
    - >-
      "The brain MCP server prescription is planned as the second (and
      final) InitStep under the fixed `brain` key. The MCP step's
      prescription.entry is derived from the scaffolded brain.aide
      bytes via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs, not from a hardcoded
      launcher constant. The canonical default scaffolded brain.aide
      launches via `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` and supports both Windows
      (cmd /c npx ...) and POSIX (npx ...) per the template helper's
      platform branching. The bundled template emits YAML null at
      every unwired slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` regardless of
      caller input — `args` is typed `(string | null)[]` and YAML null
      is the explicit, structural unwired-slot signal — and
      provisionBrain forwards those nulls verbatim through parseBrainAide
      and interpolateArgs into the derived MCP entry's args. The
      InitStep captures the file shape with null entries intact; the
      actual `.mcp.json` write happens via sync downstream, and sync's
      contract is to refuse to write null-bearing args. The hand-off
      is load-bearing: provisionBrain plans, the agent fills the
      null(s) by direct edit to brain.aide (driven verbatim by the
      integration's `config` body section prose, executed by
      `/aide:brain config`), then sync writes the result. The default
      scaffold makes no use of `${...}` substitution; the parser
      still supports `${...}` interpolation of frontmatter fields
      inside `mcpServerConfig.args` for advanced users who want to DRY
      a value across positions, but that surface is not exercised by
      the default scaffold. The step reports would-create on cold
      install (no `brain` key in `.mcp.json`), would-overwrite when
      the existing `brain` entry diverges from the derived command
      and args, and exists when the `brain` key is already present
      with an entry that matches the derived command and args."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain returns exactly TWO InitStep items in fixed
      order — the brain.aide scaffold step first, the MCP entry plan
      step second — and nothing else. There is no third step builder,
      no fourth, no seventh; the two-step shape is the contract.
      The four entry-point artifacts (playbook, study-playbook,
      update-playbook, research) and the brain root directory are NOT
      scaffolded by any step in this module's pipeline. They are
      seeded by the integration's `aide-config` body section prose at
      first `/aide:brain config`, after the user fills the null
      slot(s) and runs sync, by reading the seed-section bytes from
      brain.aide via parseBrainAide's typed keys (`playbookIndex`,
      `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and writing
      each artifact via the brain's own MCP write tool (e.g.
      `mcp__brain__write_note` for the bundled Obsidian default) at
      paths the integration prose names. provisionBrain's code path
      stops at scaffolding the bundled brain.aide template plus
      planning the `.mcp.json` brain entry; everything downstream of
      that lives in user-owned prose."
    - >-
      "provisionBrain takes no `brainPath` parameter at any layer —
      not on the orchestrator, not on any per-step builder, not on
      the bundled template helper. Its inputs are the host project
      root, the MCP config path, and the on-disk bundled template
      bytes from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`. The bundled template
      ships with YAML null at every unwired slot of
      `mcpServerConfig.args` regardless of any caller input; YAML
      null is the structural unwired-slot signal carried by the typed
      `(string | null)[]` schema, never a literal sentinel string.
      The brain root path reaches sync (and through sync, `.mcp.json`)
      only by the user (or the agent acting on their behalf via the
      integration's `config` body-section prose) editing it into
      brain.aide post-scaffold; provisionBrain re-reads it on the
