    - "Hardcoding the `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` launcher shape outside of the
      bundled default brain.aide template helper — the launcher bytes
      belong in the template helper and nowhere else in this module.
      Reintroducing an inline `obsidian-mcp` shape (the legacy launcher)
      is the same regression in different clothing: any duplication of
      launcher bytes outside the template is rejected."
    - "Scaffolding a brain.aide whose `name` value the package later
      branches on — the name field is a human-readable label for the user
      and the agent only; no code in this package may dispatch on it. Any
      code path that reads `name` to pick a backend, render
      connector-specific prose, or alter the MCP step is the in-code
      backend registry coming back through the side door."
    - >-
      "Scaffolding a brain.aide whose frontmatter contains any field beyond
      `name` and `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}` — no `connector`, no
      `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` block, no `argSlots`. Those
      fields were removed by the schema collapse; reintroducing any of
      them adds a validation-theater field with no real runtime consumer
      (sync writes `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as
      a label) and forces the parser to either ignore it or fail on it.
      The schema minimum is the schema maximum."
    - >-
      "Scaffolding a default brain.aide whose `mcpServerConfig.args`
      uses `${rootPath}` (or any other `${...}` placeholder) at any
      unwired slot, OR uses ANY literal-string sentinel —
      `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<PATH>`, `__BRAIN__`, an empty string, or any
      other shape — at any unwired slot. The unwired-slot signal is
      YAML null, full stop: `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed
      `(string | null)[]` and the bundled template emits null at every
      unwired slot. `${...}` interpolation remains the opt-in
      advanced-user surface for DRYing values across positions, but is
      not exercised by the default scaffold. Shipping any literal-string
      placeholder forces a sentinel-detection code path somewhere
      downstream, converts a structural unwired-slot signal into a
      string the tooling has to recognize, and re-couples the install
      pipeline to a 'knows what an unwired path looks like' code path;
      the structural-null approach avoids that exact tax."
    - >-
      "Replacing presence-only idempotency on brain.aide with byte
      comparison against the bundled template — brain.aide is
      seed-semantic the moment it lands; presence is the only
      idempotency signal, and any subsequent run that proposes
      would-overwrite for `.aide/config/brain.aide` shifts a
      load-bearing user-owned surface out from under the user. Same
      class of failure as the MCP server silently rewriting
      `.mcp.json`."
    - >-
      "Returning a would-overwrite step for any path under `.aide/config/`,
      regardless of which file it is — same class of failure as the MCP
      server silently rewriting `.mcp.json`. The `.aide/config/` directory
      is a load-bearing user-owned surface inherited from the root spec.
      Per-file allowlists are forbidden; the directory boundary is the
      ownership signal, and any code path that touches a file under
      `.aide/config/` after its first write — even to \"fix\" a stale
      template, land newer defaults, or tidy formatting — is rejected."
    - "Writing a brain.aide whose schema borrows intent-spec frontmatter
      conventions — no `scope`, no `outcomes`, no `intent`, no `status`
      lifecycle field. brain.aide is a config file, not an intent spec;
      conflating the two erodes the distinction every downstream agent
      depends on."
    - >-
      "Carrying a `brainPath` parameter on the orchestrator
      signature, on any per-step builder, or on the bundled template
      helper. The bundled template emits YAML null at unwired slots
      regardless of caller input; provisionBrain has no `brainPath`
      input to pass through and no branch to make on its presence or
      absence. Any `(projectRoot, brainPath?, mcpConfigPath)`-shaped
      signature, any `if (brainPath) { /* one entry */ } else { /* a
      different entry */ }` shape, and any helper-helper
      pass-through of a path argument restore the pre-pivot
      caller-driven path-routing the schema retired. This invariant
      holds across BOTH callers (`aide_init({ category: \"brain\" })`
      and `cli/init`): neither passes a brain path; cli/init is
      argument-less for brain wiring, and brain MCP entry wiring is
      delegated entirely to `/aide:brain config` on the first
      `/aide` run."
    - >-
      "Substituting any other shape for the YAML null at any unwired
      slot of `mcpServerConfig.args` — replacing null with an empty
      string, dropping the null entry from the array entirely, or
      converting it into a literal-string sentinel
      (`<BRAIN_PATH>`, `<PATH>`, `__BRAIN__`, etc.) anywhere along
      the chain (template helper, parseBrainAide, interpolateArgs,
      MCP-step InitStep). The structural unwired-slot signal is
      typed `(string | null)[]` and a YAML null is the only valid
      unwired marker; converting it into a string at any layer
      defeats sync's null-detection contract and silently lets a
      partially-wired entry land in `.mcp.json`."
    - An InitStep that writes to disk directly. provisionBrain is a
      planner; any step that touches the filesystem violates the
      architectural contract between planning helpers and the apply layer.
---

## Context

provisionBrain plans the brain-layer scaffolding both cold-install
flows need: it stands up the user-editable `brain.aide` config file
at `.aide/config/brain.aide`, and it plans the brain MCP server
entry that sync will eventually write into `.mcp.json` under the
fixed `brain` key. It writes nothing to disk — every output step is
a planning artifact the install service's apply layer turns into
bytes.

The output is exactly TWO `InitStep` records in fixed order: the
brain.aide scaffold step first (sourced from the bundled template
helper, defaulting to `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`), then the MCP
entry plan step (derived from the just-scaffolded or already-on-disk
brain.aide via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs). That is the whole
contract. The four entry-point artifacts the framework requires
(`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, `research/research.md`) and
the brain root directory itself are NOT this module's responsibility;
they are seeded by the integration's `config` body section prose at
first `/aide:brain config`, after the user fills the null slot(s)
and runs sync, by reading the seed-section bytes from brain.aide via
parseBrainAide's typed keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`,
`updatePlaybook`, `researchIndex`) and writing each artifact via the
brain's own MCP write tool at paths the integration prose names.

provisionBrain is invoked from two callers under the rework, both
consuming the same two-step output. The first is `aide_init({
category: "brain" })`, fired by `/aide:brain config` UNCONDITIONALLY
at the top of every invocation so a missing `brain.aide` self-heals
on the next call. The second is `cli/init`, which now scaffolds the
bundled `brain.aide` template at cold install (this is new — cli/init
was previously brain-agnostic). Both callers apply step 1
(write `brain.aide` when absent) idempotently. Neither applies step
2: cli/init is at the install boundary and sync owns `.mcp.json`
brain writes downstream; `/aide:brain config` defers the MCP write
to sync as well, after the integration prose fills the null slot.
Provision plans, the agent fills, sync writes — that is the
load-bearing hand-off.

The reason this module shrunk to two steps is a structural failure
mode the previous shape carried. The pre-rework pipeline kept seven
InitSteps and reached for a `PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3` constant plus a
`resolveBrainRoot` helper to read `args[3]` out of the parsed
brain.aide so it could plan the four entry-point artifact writes.
That violated the root spec's per-integration ownership of arg-index
slot semantics — the package introspecting which slot is the path is
the in-code backend registry coming back through the slot surface.
Worse, an earlier shape used a literal-string sentinel
(`<BRAIN_PATH>`) at the unwired path slot; the sentinel propagated
byte-for-byte into both `brain.aide` and `.mcp.json`, the drift
detector returned `ok` because the bytes agreed, the brain MCP server
failed silently at launch, and the user had no signal that anything
was wrong (this regression was confirmed in `D:\Code\Me\aide-fresh`
during smoke testing). The rework fixes both at once: provisionBrain
becomes backend-blind at the slot level, the unwired-slot signal
becomes a structurally-typed YAML null in `mcpServerConfig.args`, and
slot-semantics, file-path, and write-tool-name knowledge ALL move
into user-owned prose where each integration owns its own surface.

The brain.aide path lives under `.aide/config/`, the host's
user-owned configuration directory inherited from the root spec.
The ownership rule is directory-wide: any file scaffolded into
`.aide/config/` is owned by the user the moment it lands. No
subsequent `aide_init` or `aide_upgrade` run overwrites, patches, or
migrates anything inside that directory. provisionBrain inherits
that structural rule rather than restating it per file.

## Strategy

**Two planning steps in fixed order: brain.aide scaffold → MCP
entry plan. Nothing else.** The shape is the contract. brain.aide
is structural — the file must exist or the parser has nothing to
read and every downstream consumer (sync, buildBrainState, the
integration's config prose) blocks. The MCP entry is structural —
sync's contract is to write whatever prescription is on the plan
verbatim, and refuse if any arg is null. Those two things and only
those two things are what install-boundary code can decide without
introspecting integration-specific structure. Anything beyond them
— the brain root directory, the four entry-point artifacts — needs
to know where files go inside the user's chosen brain, and that
knowledge is per-integration prose, not package code. A third step,
fourth step, or seven-step shape under any rationale ("we need a
step for the brain root," "we need a step per entry-point artifact")
restores the regression class the rework was built to close.

**The four entry-point artifacts move to the integration's `config`
body section prose, NOT to a step builder under any rename.** The
prior pipeline kept `buildBrainRootStep`, `buildPlaybookStep`,
`buildStudyPlaybookStep`, `buildUpdatePlaybookStep`, and
`buildResearchStep` because each artifact needed bytes from a
brain.aide body section AND a destination path inside the brain
root. The destination path is the problem: knowing where
`coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` lives requires knowing the
brain root, and the brain root lives at `args[3]` for the bundled
Obsidian default but at a different index for any other integration
that ships its own template. The previous architect's plan tried
to keep the seven-step shape under the new no-`brainPath` constraint
by introducing `PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3` and a `resolveBrainRoot`
helper that read `args[3]` to extract the brain root. That violates
the root spec's invariant that slot semantics live exclusively in
the per-integration `config` body section. The integration prose,
in contrast, knows everything: it owns the slot index, the artifact
paths, AND the brain's own MCP write tool name. So the four artifact
writes flow from the integration prose at first `/aide:brain config`,
which reads each seed section's bytes via parseBrainAide's typed
keys (`playbookIndex`, `studyPlaybook`, `updatePlaybook`,
`researchIndex`) and writes each to its named path via the brain's
MCP write tool. provisionBrain stays backend-blind at the slot
level, and restoring any of the five retired step builders under
any rename is the structural regression this spec forbids.

**provisionBrain takes no `brainPath` parameter, no slot-position
constant, and no `args[N]` introspection at any layer.** The
orchestrator signature, the per-step builders, and the bundled
template helper all take only the inputs they actually need: the
host project root, the MCP config path, and the bundled template
bytes. No `PATH_SLOT_INDEX` constant. No `args[N]` switch. No
`resolveBrainRoot`-style helper. No matcher recognizing
token-shaped strings at any index. No `argSlots` frontmatter
field. The MCP step reads `mcpServerConfig.args` only as opaque
bytes — passing them byte-for-byte through parseBrainAide and
interpolateArgs into the prescription, including any YAML null
entries unchanged. The package's introspection of args is exactly
zero; per-integration ownership of slot semantics is what keeps
this module portable across future integrations whose path slot
sits at a different index, whose unwired slot is a token rather
than a path, or whose schema demands no path at all.

**The unwired-slot signal is YAML null, not a literal-string
sentinel.** `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`,
the typing is the contract, and YAML null is the explicit,
structural unwired-slot signal. The prior shape used a
literal-string placeholder at the same slot, which had a
load-bearing failure mode: the placeholder string was byte-stable
across reads, so an MCP step computed against it would match an
existing `.mcp.json` entry that also carried the placeholder
verbatim — the drift check passed, sync wrote the placeholder, and
the brain MCP server failed silently at launch until the user
noticed (this regression was confirmed in `D:\Code\Me\aide-fresh`).
Structural null closes that trap at the schema layer: sync's
contract is to refuse to write null-bearing args, so a
partially-wired brain configuration cannot silently land in
`.mcp.json`. The package never has to recognize a string the
template chose as the "unwired" marker; the typed schema carries
that signal directly. The bundled template ships YAML null at
unwired slots regardless of caller; provisionBrain forwards the
null verbatim through parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs into the
MCP-step InitStep, and never substitutes, drops, or coerces it at
any layer.

**Dual-caller architecture, identical output, divergent apply.**
provisionBrain returns the same two-step plan regardless of caller.
What differs is which subset of the plan each caller's apply layer
runs:
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })` is the brain-category entry
point fired by `/aide:brain config` UNCONDITIONALLY at the top of
every invocation; its apply path runs step 1 idempotently
(self-healing a deleted brain.aide), and step 2 is staged for sync
but not written here — the integration prose runs sync after
filling the null slot. `cli/init` is the cold-install CLI; its
apply path runs step 1 only, never step 2, because sync owns
`.mcp.json` brain entry writes and cli/init runs at the install
boundary where sync has not yet been invoked. The previous handoff
captured cli/init as brain-agnostic; this rework makes it the
cold-install entry point for the bundled brain.aide template
specifically, while keeping the brain MCP entry write deferred to
sync. cli/init reaching into provisionBrain to apply step 2 would
re-couple the cold-install path to brain MCP wiring — exactly the
coupling sync was placed to remove.

**Presence-only idempotency on the brain.aide step is the
load-bearing rule that makes the unconditional `aide_init` call
safe.** brain.aide returns `exists` whenever the file is present
at `.aide/config/brain.aide`, regardless of byte drift from the
bundled template. The user owns the bytes the moment the file
lands — they may have filled the null slot, edited the orientation
section to fit their workflow, or replaced an entire seed section
with their own prose. Byte-comparison idempotency would surface
each customization as `would-overwrite` and degrade the
unconditional self-heal into a continual nag, eventually retraining
the user to reflexively confirm and clobber their own work.
Presence-only idempotency does the opposite: a customized
brain.aide is left alone forever, and only a genuinely-absent file
(deleted by accident, missing because a prior run was interrupted)
returns `would-create` and re-seeds the bundled template. The
ownership rule applies directory-wide to `.aide/config/`: the
package never returns `would-overwrite` for any path under that
directory under any rationale, including "fixing" a stale template
or landing newer defaults. The MCP step uses canonical-derived
idempotency instead because its bytes belong to the package, not
the user: the expected entry is computed from the current
brain.aide via parseBrainAide + interpolateArgs and compared
against the existing `.mcp.json` brain entry. Divergence is
`would-overwrite`; that rule applies only to the MCP step, not to
brain.aide.

**brain.aide is the single source of truth for launcher bytes; the
MCP step is derived, never inlined.** The MCP step's prescription
is computed by reading the just-scaffolded (or already-on-disk)
brain.aide via parseBrainAide for the on-disk path, or
parseBrainAideFromString for the cold-install path where the
template bytes have not yet hit disk, then running interpolateArgs
to resolve any `${...}` substitutions inside `mcpServerConfig.args`.
No code path constructs the brain MCP entry inline from a hardcoded
launcher shape, a hardcoded `@bitbonsai/mcpvault` reference, or any
other in-code launcher constant. The default scaffold makes no use
of `${...}` substitution; the parser still supports it for advanced
users who want to DRY a frontmatter value across positions, but
that surface is opt-in and unexercised by the bundled template.
The two parser entry points yield structurally identical fields,
so the cold-install and on-disk branches share the same downstream
interpolation pass. Inlining a launcher anywhere — under any
rename, under any "temporary" rationale — restores the duplication
the schema collapse already retired.

**Migration semantics for pre-rework brain.aide files are strict
failure, not auto-rewrite.** Cold installs land in the new shape
automatically — the bundled template carries all six marker-bounded
body sections from day one with YAML null at the unwired slot. Any
pre-rework shape (heading-based body, four-section marker-bounded
body with retired marker names, a brain.aide carrying a
literal-string placeholder rather than YAML null at the path slot,
a brain.aide whose frontmatter still names retired fields like
`connector` or `rootPath`) surfaces as `malformed-body` or
`malformed-frontmatter` from parseBrainAide because the parser's
grammar is closed. provisionBrain does not silently patch the file
under `.aide/config/` to rename markers, fill missing sections, or
convert literal placeholders to null — that would violate the
directory-wide ownership rule and re-introduce a "package knows
how the user's config should look" code path. The user hand-edits
their brain.aide to align with the new shape. The strict-failure
path is intentional, and until the user completes the migration
the upstream parse failure surfaces as the actionable error rather
than a silent in-place mutation.

**Code identifiers and inline prose stay storage-agnostic.** No
identifier in this module's code — function name, variable name,
parameter name, file path constant, JSDoc word, log string — uses
any of the forbidden tokens. The two structural concepts the
module handles are "the brain config file scaffold" and "the brain
MCP entry plan"; how the brain itself manifests in the user's
chosen storage is brain.aide's concern, not this module's. The
bundled template helper folder name `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` is
the only allowed exception; it describes bundled template content
rather than a code-level dispatch axis. Methodology guidance about
how the agent navigates or maintains the brain (link-crawling
protocols, search-vs-skill routing, entry-point traversal rules,
playbook-maintenance recipes) lives only in the bundled template's
seed section bytes (which become user-owned the moment they land)
and in pipeline prompts — never in inline string constants in this
module's code. The carve-out applies to the bytes inside the
bundled template's body sections, not to provisionBrain's code
surface.

## Good examples

**Cold install on a brand-new project, via cli/init.** The user
runs `npx aidemd-mcp init`. cli/init's apply pipeline reaches the
brain category and calls provisionBrain. The host has no
`.aide/config/brain.aide` and no `.mcp.json` brain entry yet.
provisionBrain returns exactly two InitStep items:

**Step 1 — Brain config (brain.aide).** Status: `would-create`.
Category: `brain`. FilePath: `.aide/config/brain.aide` inside the
host project root. Content: the full canonical brain.aide bytes
from `obsidianBrainAideTemplate()`, which on POSIX has frontmatter
shaped like:

```
---
name: "obsidian"
mcpServerConfig:
  command: "npx"
  args:
    - "-y"
    - "@bitbonsai/mcpvault"
    - "--vault"
    -
---
```

The bare trailing `-` is YAML null — the parser yields JS `null`
at `args[3]`. The body carries six marker-bounded sections in
fixed order (orientation, config, playbook-index, study-playbook,
update-playbook, research-index), with package-authored bytes the
user owns the moment the file lands.

**Step 2 — MCP config (brain).** Status: `would-create`. Category:
`mcp`. Prescription derived from the Step 1 content bytes via
parseBrainAideFromString + interpolateArgs. The prescription's
`args` carries the YAML null at index 3 verbatim through the
parser and the interpolator:

```
{
  key: "brain",
  entry: {
    command: "npx",
    args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--vault", null]
  }
}
```

cli/init's apply layer writes step 1 silently (the file is
absent), and STOPS — it does not apply step 2. Sync owns
`.mcp.json` brain writes. The terminal warning at the end of
cli/init routes the user to `/aide:brain config` to fill the null
slot and run sync.

**Self-heal recovery: brain.aide accidentally deleted, user runs
`/aide:brain config`.** The router unconditionally calls
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })`. provisionBrain returns:

- **Step 1 — Brain config (brain.aide).** Status: `would-create`.
  Same content as the cold-install case (bundled template bytes
  with YAML null at args[3]).
- **Step 2 — MCP config (brain).** Status depends on the existing
  `.mcp.json`. If a stale brain entry from the prior install is
  still on disk and matches the prescription derived from the
  re-scaffolded template (e.g. it also carries null at index 3),
  the step is `exists`. If the existing brain entry diverges (it
  carried the user's filled-in path before brain.aide was deleted,
  or has been edited by hand), the step is `would-overwrite` and
  the prescription drives the agent toward re-filling and
  re-syncing.

The apply layer writes step 1 silently, the integration's config
prose runs sync after the user fills the null slot, and the four
entry-point artifacts at `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`,
`coding-playbook/update-playbook.md`, and `research/research.md`
are seeded by that same prose via the brain's MCP write tool
(`mcp__brain__write_note` for the bundled Obsidian default),
reading the seed-section bytes from brain.aide via parseBrainAide's
typed keys.

**Steady-state: brain wired, user re-runs `/aide:brain config`
with no arguments.** The router still unconditionally calls
`aide_init({ category: "brain" })`. provisionBrain returns:

- **Step 1 — Brain config (brain.aide).** Status: `exists`.
  Presence-only check: the file is on disk at
  `.aide/config/brain.aide`, regardless of whether the user has
  edited the orientation section, customized the config flow,
  filled the null slot, or rewritten any seed section. No content
  field on this step.
- **Step 2 — MCP config (brain).** Status: `exists`. The
  prescription derived from the current brain.aide (which now
  carries the user's absolute path string at args[3], no nulls)
  matches the `brain` entry already in `.mcp.json` byte-for-byte.

The apply layer writes nothing; the integration's config prose
short-circuits on the "already-wired" branch and emits a STOP
message.

**MCP drift: user hand-edited `.mcp.json` brain entry.**
provisionBrain returns step 1 as `exists` and step 2 as
`would-overwrite`. The prescription on step 2 reflects what the
brain MCP entry SHOULD be per the current brain.aide; the apply
layer (driven by the integration prose) prompts the user, and on
acceptance sync writes the corrected entry.

## Bad examples

**A third InitStep that writes a coding-playbook entry-point
artifact.** The output array carries three steps:

- Step 1: brain.aide scaffold (correct).
- Step 2: writes `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md` into the
  brain root, sourced from the parsed brain.aide's `playbookIndex`
  seed section.
- Step 3: MCP entry plan.

This restores the retired `buildPlaybookStep` regression class
under any rename: `seedPlaybook`, `writePlaybookArtifact`,
`scaffoldPlaybookIndex`. The same regression covers the four other
retired builders (brain root directory creation, study-playbook,
update-playbook, research). Entry-point artifact writes need to
know the brain root, the artifact paths inside it, and the brain's
write-tool name — knowledge that lives only in the integration's
`config` body section. provisionBrain emits exactly two steps:
brain.aide scaffold + MCP entry plan. Anything else is rejected
regardless of identifier.

**A `PATH_SLOT_INDEX = 3` constant in `index.ts`.** Any module-local
named integer that hardcodes a slot position (`PATH_SLOT_INDEX`,
`BRAIN_ROOT_ARG`, `VAULT_PATH_INDEX` — the spelling does not
matter) introspects which `mcpServerConfig.args` slot means what.
That knowledge belongs to the per-integration `config` body
section, not the package. The bundled Obsidian default puts the
brain root at `args[3]`; a future Mem0 integration would put an
API token at `args[2]`; a future no-path integration would put
nothing in any path slot. Encoding `3` (or any other index) in
this module's code is the in-code backend registry coming back
through the slot surface.

**A `resolveBrainRoot(args)` helper that reads `args[3]`.** The
helper takes the parsed `mcpServerConfig.args`, fishes out the
element at index 3, and returns it as the brain root. Any variant
— `extractBrainPath`, `getBrainRoot`, `pickPathSlot`,
`readBrainRootFromArgs`, a destructure like `const [, , , brainRoot]
= args` — is the same regression. The package introspects nothing
about which arg slot is which; `args` is opaque bytes that
provisionBrain forwards verbatim through parseBrainAide and
interpolateArgs into the MCP-step prescription. A function whose
job is to extract a brain root from args has no place in this
module under any name.

**A `brainPath` parameter on the orchestrator signature.** The
signature shaped `(projectRoot, brainPath?, mcpConfigPath)`
restores the pre-pivot caller-driven path-routing the schema
collapse retired. It does not matter whether the parameter is
optional or required, propagated to the bundled template helper,
or used inside an `if (brainPath) { ... } else { ... }` branch in
either consumer. provisionBrain takes only `projectRoot` and
`mcpConfigPath`; the bundled template helper takes only the
inputs it needs to emit its bytes (no `brainPath` either). The
brain root reaches sync only via the user (or the agent acting on
their behalf via the integration's `config` body section prose)
editing it into brain.aide post-scaffold.

**An `argSlots` frontmatter field declaring "this index is a
path".** A brain.aide whose frontmatter looks like:

```
---
name: "obsidian"
mcpServerConfig:
  command: "npx"
  args: ["-y", "@bitbonsai/mcpvault", "--vault", null]
argSlots:
  - kind: "literal"
  - kind: "literal"
  - kind: "literal"
  - kind: "path"
---
```

is wrong. `argSlots` is the same regression as `PATH_SLOT_INDEX`
sitting on the user-owned surface instead of in code. The package
would have to read it (introspecting which slot is which), the
parser would have to validate it (more validation theater), and
slot semantics would now live in the frontmatter rather than in
the integration's `config` body section. The schema minimum is
the schema maximum: `name` and `mcpServerConfig` only. Slot
semantics live exclusively in the per-integration `config` body
section prose.

**The bundled template emitting a literal-string sentinel instead
of YAML null at the unwired slot.** A frontmatter shape carrying
`"<BRAIN_PATH>"`, `"<PATH>"`, `"__BRAIN__"`, `""`, or `"${rootPath}"`
at the unwired slot is rejected. The unwired-slot signal is YAML
null, full stop: `mcpServerConfig.args` is typed `(string | null)[]`
and the typing is the contract. A literal-string sentinel
re-creates the byte-stable failure mode the rework was built to
close — a `.mcp.json` brain entry that already carries the
sentinel verbatim byte-matches an MCP step computed against a
re-scaffolded sentinel-bearing brain.aide, the drift check passes,
sync writes the sentinel, and the brain MCP server fails to
launch until the user notices. The same rejection applies to
dropping the entry from the array entirely.

**cli/init applying step 2 to write the brain MCP entry directly
to `.mcp.json`.** cli/init's apply pipeline runs through the brain
category, calls provisionBrain, and writes both InitSteps to disk
— including step 2's prescription, which it merges into
`.mcp.json` under the `brain` key. Wrong on two levels:
`.mcp.json` brain writes belong to sync, which has the
null-refusal contract that catches partially-wired configurations
loudly; cli/init's apply layer has no such precondition, so it
would happily write a null-bearing brain entry that fails silently
at IDE launch. cli/init applies step 1 only — write brain.aide
when absent — and STOPS. Sync, invoked downstream by the
integration's `config` prose after the user fills the null slot,
is the only mutation surface for the brain MCP entry.

**provisionBrain "defensively" replacing a null at plan time.**
A code path that walks the parsed `mcpServerConfig.args`, replaces
each null with a fallback (the user's home directory, an empty
string, a default path string), and emits the substituted args
into the MCP step's prescription is wrong. The null is the
structural unwired-slot signal — provisionBrain forwards it
verbatim through parseBrainAide and interpolateArgs, and sync
(downstream) refuses to write null-bearing args. Defensive
null-replacement re-couples the install pipeline to a "knows what
an unwired slot looks like" code path and masks user wiring
mistakes that the sync-refusal surfaces loudly.

**A step returning `would-overwrite` for `.aide/config/brain.aide`.**
A code path that byte-compares the on-disk brain.aide against the
bundled template and returns `would-overwrite` when they diverge
silently reverts the user's filled-in path back to YAML null on
the next install. The `.aide/config/` directory is a directory-wide
user-owned boundary; per-file allowlists are forbidden, and no
code path in this module returns `would-overwrite` for any path
inside it under any rationale, including "fixing" a stale
template or landing newer defaults. brain.aide uses presence-only
idempotency: present means `exists`, absent means `would-create`.

